  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14095163/assets/ec4af1357e75dcee6d7d5412467c2ee2ea9e54fa38fb6e83817b0ae909c529aa.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(2+3{e}^{-x})\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2+3{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2+3{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2+3{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=2x-3{e}^{-x}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & =2(0)-3{e}^{-0}+C \\\\ 2 & =-3+C \\\\ C & =2+3 \\\\ C & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2x-3{e}^{-x}+5 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4x-6{e}^{-x}+10 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{4x-6{e}^{-x}+10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=\\sqrt{4x-6{e}^{-x}+10}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\sqrt{4(3)-6{e}^{-3}+10} \\\\ v & =\\sqrt{22-6{e}^{-3}} \\\\ v & \u22484.7\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.",
